questions.stem:
A badge printer tries to use a colon-form switch as a value, and the compiler refuses the file. What is the actual reason?
int code = 2;
String label = switch (code) {
    case 1: "one";
    case 2: "two";
    default: "many";
};
System.out.println(label);

A report builder splices a switch expression straight into the text it prints, so no variable ever holds the grade. What does the printer output?
int level = 2;
System.out.println("grade=" + switch (level) {
    case 1 -> "A";
    case 2 -> "B";
    default -> "C";
});

A subscription summary writes a value-form switch that names only two of the three tiers, and the build fails. What does the compiler say and why?
enum Tier { BASIC, PLUS, PRO }
Tier t = Tier.PLUS;
String label = switch (t) {
    case BASIC -> "basic";
    case PRO -> "pro";
};
System.out.println(label);

A converter tries to hand a value back from inside a rule block using return, and the compiler objects. What is going wrong?
int n = 2;
String s = switch (n) {
    case 2 -> { return "two"; }
    default -> "many";
};
System.out.println(s);

A logging rule block runs a print and builds a string, but the build fails before anything runs. What is the compiler's objection?
int n = 1;
String s = switch (n) {
    case 1 -> { String t = "one"; System.out.print("log "); }
    default -> "many";
};
System.out.println(s);

A guard refuses to run a diagnostic in an unsafe mode, and the mode chosen is the one it refuses. What happens?
enum Mode { FAST, SAFE }
Mode m = Mode.SAFE;
String tag = switch (m) {
    case FAST -> "fast";
    case SAFE -> { throw new IllegalStateException("unsafe"); }
};
System.out.println(tag);

A formatter adds an arrow rule to the end of a colon-form switch while converting it rule by rule, and the build stops. What has gone wrong?
int k = 1;
switch (k) {
    case 1: System.out.print("a"); break;
    default -> System.out.print("b");
}
System.out.println(" end");

A bonus switch shares one declared variable across two labels, and one of them can be entered without passing the declaration. What does the compiler report?
int k = 1;
switch (k) {
    case 1:
    case 2:
        int bonus = 10;
        System.out.print(bonus);
        break;
    case 3:
        System.out.print(bonus);
        break;
}
System.out.println(" end");

A retry notice names one outcome and keeps a fallback underneath it with no break in between. What does the printer output?
int k = 1;
switch (k) {
    case 1: System.out.print("one ");
    default: System.out.print("fallback ");
}
System.out.println("end");

Three labels sit in one switch with the fallback in the middle and nothing between them to stop the fall. What does the printer output?
int k = 1;
switch (k) {
    case 1: System.out.print("one ");
    default: System.out.print("any ");
    case 9: System.out.print("nine ");
}
System.out.println("end");

A scanner inside a case body breaks out of its own loop, and the case has more work after that loop. What does the printer output?
int k = 1;
switch (k) {
    case 1:
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            if (i == 1) break;
            System.out.print("t" + i + " ");
        }
        System.out.print("after ");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.print("two ");
}
System.out.println("end");

A loop switches on its counter, and one pass short-circuits the rest of the case body. What does the printer output?
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    switch (i) {
        case 2: System.out.print("hit "); continue;
        default: System.out.print("d");
    }
    System.out.print("after" + i + " ");
}
System.out.println("end");

A search loop carries a label, and a switch inside it uses that label to give up entirely. What does the printer output?
int k = 0;
search:
for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    switch (k) {
        case 0:
            if (i == 2) break search;
            System.out.print(i);
            break;
        default:
            System.out.print("x");
    }
}
System.out.println(" end");

A tier printer groups constants into two arrow rules that share one value, and the build refuses it. What is the compiler's reason?
int k = 1;
switch (k) {
    case 1, 2 -> System.out.print("low ");
    case 2, 3 -> System.out.print("high ");
}
System.out.println("end");

A shape switch picks a rule by the remainder of the input, and the chosen rule computes its value before handing it back. What does the printer output?
int n = 7;
String shape = switch (n % 3) {
    case 0 -> {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            sb.append(i);
        }
        yield sb.toString();
    }
    default -> {
        int q = n / 3;
        yield "q" + q;
    }
};
System.out.println(shape);

A lamp report uses an enum selector but leaves one label without a break, so the next body runs too. What does the printer output?
enum Lamp { OFF, ON, AUTO }
Lamp l = Lamp.ON;
switch (l) {
    case OFF: System.out.print("dark ");
    case ON: System.out.print("lit "); break;
    case AUTO: System.out.print("smart ");
}
System.out.println("end");

A developer writes rules whose bodies are values but forgets to say where the value should go, and the build fails. What is the problem?
int k = 1;
switch (k) {
    case 1 -> "one";
    default -> "many";
}
System.out.println("end");

An arrow rule is given a block so it can hold a whole loop, and the loop is the only work in that case. What does the printer output?
int k = 1;
switch (k) {
    case 1 -> {
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            System.out.print(i);
        }
    }
    default -> System.out.print("x");
}
System.out.println(" end");

A settings lookup passes a null selector and the switch offers a label for exactly that case. What does the printer output?
String pick = null;
String label = switch (pick) {
    case null -> "absent";
    case "x" -> "ex";
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(label);

A code picker lists two arrow rules and no default, and the value it is given is in neither list. What does the printer output?
int k = 9;
switch (k) {
    case 1 -> System.out.print("one ");
    case 2 -> System.out.print("two ");
}
System.out.println("end");

A two-pass loop puts a switch in its body, and the last label relies on falling out of the switch rather than breaking. What does the printer output?
int hits = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
    switch (i) {
        case 0: System.out.print("a");
        case 1: System.out.print("b"); break;
    }
    hits++;
}
System.out.println(" " + hits);

A job row holds one enum constant and a guard tests it against a named constant with the identity operator. What does the printer output?
enum Status { NEW, DONE }
Status s = Status.NEW;
boolean open = s == Status.NEW;
System.out.println(open);

A size chart switches on the declaration index of the constant instead of the constant itself. What does the printer output?
enum Size { SMALL, MEDIUM, LARGE }
Size s = Size.MEDIUM;
String tag = switch (s.ordinal()) {
    case 0 -> "xs";
    case 1 -> "md";
    case 2 -> "lg";
    default -> "?";
};
System.out.println(tag);

A rota compares two shifts to see which one comes later in the day, and the enum supplies the comparison. What does the printer output?
enum Shift { MORNING, AFTERNOON, NIGHT }
Shift a = Shift.NIGHT;
Shift b = Shift.MORNING;
System.out.println(a.compareTo(b) > 0);

A settings screen turns typed text into a mode constant and supplies a substitute when the text is not one it knows. What does the printer output?
enum Mode { FAST, SLOW }
String typed = "turbo";
Mode m;
try {
    m = Mode.valueOf(typed);
} catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
    m = Mode.SLOW;
}
System.out.println(m);

A settings audit walks an enum's constant list by index and checks each entry against one named constant. What does the printer output?
enum Level { LOW, MED, HIGH }
int found = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < Level.values().length; i++) {
    if (Level.values()[i] == Level.MED) {
        found++;
    }
}
System.out.println(found);

A permissions screen holds a set of granted capabilities and asks about one that was never granted. What does the printer output?
import java.util.EnumSet;
enum Flag { READ, WRITE, EXEC }
EnumSet<Flag> granted = EnumSet.of(Flag.READ, Flag.EXEC);
System.out.println(granted.contains(Flag.WRITE));

A quota switch stores its answer as a side effect and hands back the stored number as the rule's value. What does the printer output?
int remaining = 0;
int note = switch (2) {
    case 1 -> 1;
    case 2 -> remaining = 5;
    default -> 0;
};
System.out.println(note + " " + remaining);

A dispatcher switches on an expression that increments the picker as a side effect, and the value is observed afterwards. What does the printer output?
int pick = 2;
String label = switch (pick++) {
    case 1 -> "one";
    case 2 -> "two";
    default -> "many";
};
System.out.println(label + " " + pick);

A loop asks a switch expression for a contribution on every pass and adds each answer into a total. What does the printer output?
int i = 0;
int total = 0;
while (i < 4) {
    total = total + switch (i) {
        case 0, 1 -> 1;
        case 2, 3 -> 10;
        default -> 0;
    };
    i++;
}
System.out.println(total);
